.TH LIBESDM_EGD_CLIENT 3 "2026-10-19" "ESDM @VERSION@" "ESDM Manual"
.SH NAME
libesdm_egd_client \- client library for the ESDM EGD interface
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <esdm/esdm_egd_client.h>
.PP
.B pkg\-config \-\-cflags \-\-libs esdm_egd_client
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B libesdm_egd_client
speaks the EGD (Entropy Gathering Daemon) protocol to
.BR esdm\-server (8),
which serves it when started with
.B \-\-egd_socket
or through the shipped EGD socket units.
.PP
The reason to use this rather than
.BR libesdm_rpc_client (3)
is reach: the EGD interface needs nothing but a single UNIX domain stream
socket \- no protobuf, no shared memory, no second socket \- so it works in
places the RPC interface cannot go, such as a chroot that can be handed
exactly one socket. The price is the protocol: it is a legacy one that cannot
transfer more than
.B ESDM_EGD_MAX_TRANSFER
(255) bytes per request, though this client splits larger requests
transparently \- the commands of a blocking read of up to 64 KiB go out at
once and their answers are read after, one round trip per 64 KiB rather than
per transfer. Nor can it express prediction resistance: the ESDM offers that
with a read command of its own on the same socket, which
.BR esdm_egd_client_get_random_pr ()
and
.BR esdm_egd_client_get_random_pr_nonblock ()
use, see
.BR esdm\-server (8).
.PP
In its network modes, the library speaks the same protocol over TCP to an
.BR esdm\-egd\-net\-server (8),
which serves the EGD protocol of the ESDM on another host \- see
.BR "NETWORK MODES" .
.SS Clients and concurrency
A client owns exactly one connection. The EGD protocol carries no request
identifiers \- responses are matched to requests purely by their order on the
stream \- so all operations of one client are serialized against each other and
one request is in flight at a time. An application that wants concurrency uses
several clients.
.PP
That serialization survives a caller that does not: the lock behind it is a
robust one, so a thread killed in the middle of an operation hands the client
to the next caller instead of wedging it. The connection it left at an unknown
offset of the command stream is dropped.
.SS Reconnecting
All operations are thread safe and reconnect on their own:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
A client whose initial connect failed \- because the ESDM is not up yet \- is
still handed back usable and connects on first use. A consumer initialized
before the daemon exists, such as an OpenSSL provider loaded during boot, keeps
working once the daemon arrives.
.IP \(bu 2
A transfer that fails halfway leaves the peer's parser at an unknown offset, so
the connection is dropped rather than reused and the operation is retried once
on a fresh one. A daemon restart between two requests is therefore invisible to
the caller.
.IP \(bu 2
A descriptor inherited across
.BR fork (2)
refers to the parent's connection, and two processes interleaving commands on
it would desynchronize the stream. A fork handler closes every inherited
connection in the child; the next operation there opens one of its own. The
TLS state of an inherited TLS connection is left to leak in the child, as a
parent thread may have been using it at the fork. Should the fork have bypassed
the handler \- a direct
.BR clone (2),
say \- the client notices at its next use and reconnects then.
.IP \(bu 2
A server of the network closes a connection that was idle for long, which a
TCP connection only shows as the end of its stream \- data written into it
afterwards is lost. Before every request on a network connection, the client
checks whether anything arrived on it unasked, and replaces it if so: an
insertion, which is not answered, would otherwise be lost silently.
.IP \(bu 2
An application may close the client's descriptor behind its back \- OpenSSH
runs
.BR closefrom (3)
on its way into the pre\-authentication child \- and the number may be taken by
whatever it opens next. Before every operation the client confirms that the
descriptor still is the connection it made, by the socket cookie or, where the
kernel has none, by the peer's address. One that is not is left to the
application, never closed by the client, and a fresh connection is made.
.RE
.SH NETWORK MODES
A client allocated with
.BR esdm_egd_client_alloc_net ()
connects to an
.BR esdm\-egd\-net\-server (8).
Everything said above holds there as well \- one connection per client, the
serialized operations, the reconnect and the retry, the fork handling \- and
the server treats every client of the network as unprivileged.
.TP
.B Plain TCP
The protocol as it is, neither encrypted nor authenticated: only for a network
whose transport is protected underneath, by IPsec or WireGuard, say. Once
connected, a client keeps the
.B SYSCALL DISCIPLINE
below, so a sandboxed process may use it as it uses the UNIX domain socket,
connected before it enters its sandbox. The server closes a connection idle
for its
.BR \-\-idle_timeout ,
though, which such a process does not survive.
.TP
.B TLS
TLS 1.3 with mutual authentication: the client presents its certificate and
verifies the server's chain against its trust anchors, and that the
certificate is issued for the name it expects \- a DNS name, or an IPv4 or IPv6
address. With CRLs, the server's chain is checked as described in
.B REVOCATION
of
.BR esdm\-egd\-net\-server (8).
The TLS stack is that of the crypto backend the ESDM is built with \- OpenSSL,
GnuTLS or Botan. The library does not link it: the first client in TLS mode
loads it with the TLS module of the library,
.IR esdm\-egd\-tls.so ,
so that a process that never allocates such a client has no TLS stack loaded,
which would make syscalls of its own when it is loaded and unloaded. TLS mode
does not keep the syscall discipline: the TLS stack sends and receives with
.BR send (2)
and
.BR recv (2)
and uses whatever else it needs. A sandboxed process uses the
.BR esdm\-egd\-net\-relay (8)
through its UNIX domain socket instead.
.PP
The TLS files are read when the client is allocated. Each later connection
checks them for a change \- their inode, size and modification time \- and
loads them anew when they changed: a renewed certificate is taken up by the
next connection. Files that do not load completely leave the client with what
was loaded before, and the error is logged. The clients of a process with the
same files share what they loaded.
.PP
The network modes exist in a build with the meson option
.BR egd\-net ,
the TLS mode with
.B egd\-net\-tls
and a crypto backend with a TLS stack;
.BR esdm_egd_client_alloc_net ()
reports
.B \-EOPNOTSUPP
otherwise.
.SH FUNCTIONS
.TP
.BI "int esdm_egd_client_alloc(struct esdm_egd_client **" client ", const char *" socket_path ", unsigned int " timeout_ms ");"
Allocate a client and connect it. Release it with
.BR esdm_egd_client_free ().
.IP
.I socket_path
selects the EGD socket; NULL or the empty string takes the path from the
.B ESDM_EGD_SOCKET
environment variable, and failing that the socket path this ESDM was built
with.
.IP
.I timeout_ms
bounds how long the client waits for the peer in one step \- the connection
setup, or one transfer of a request or its response \- before treating it as
failed. The socket is non\-blocking and every wait is a poll of its own, so this
covers the connect as well, which a receive timeout cannot. 0 selects a default
generous enough for the blocking read command to wait for an ESDM that is not
operational yet; unreasonably large values are capped.
.IP
Returns 0 on success, and a negative value only when no client could be
allocated. A failure to connect is not an error here \- it is reported through
the ESDM logger and the client is usable regardless.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_egd_client_alloc_net(struct esdm_egd_client **" client ", const struct esdm_egd_client_net_config *" cfg ");"
Allocate a client of an
.BR esdm\-egd\-net\-server (8)
and connect it, as
.BR esdm_egd_client_alloc ().
.I cfg
is read during the call only:
.RS
.TP
.B size_t size
.B sizeof(struct esdm_egd_client_net_config)
of the caller. Fields are only ever appended to the structure, and the library
takes those
.I size
covers and zero for the rest: initialize the whole structure with zeros.
.TP
.B enum esdm_egd_client_net_mode mode
.B ESDM_EGD_CLIENT_NET_TLS
or
.BR ESDM_EGD_CLIENT_NET_PLAIN .
There is no default.
.TP
.B const char *address
The server: "host:port", "[IPv6 address]:port", or either without the port
for the one the ESDM was built with (meson option
.BR egd\-net\-port ).
Resolved with
.BR getaddrinfo (3)
at every connection; its addresses are tried in turn.
.TP
.B unsigned int timeout_ms
As for
.BR esdm_egd_client_alloc (),
the connection setup being the TCP connection and the TLS handshake.
.TP
.B const char *cert_file, *key_file, *ca_file
TLS only: the certificate of the client, PEM, optionally followed by its
intermediate certificates; its key, PEM, unencrypted PKCS#8; the trust anchors
of the server, PEM. The client's certificate has to chain to them as well.
.TP
.B const char *server_name
TLS only: the name the server's certificate has to be issued for; NULL takes
the host of
.IR address .
.TP
.B const char *const *crl_files
TLS only: NULL, or up to
.B ESDM_EGD_CLIENT_NET_MAX_CRL
CRL files, PEM, NULL\-terminated.
.TP
.B unsigned int crl_flags
TLS only:
.B ESDM_EGD_CLIENT_NET_CRL_CHECK_ALL
requires CRLs for the CA certificates of the server's chain as well,
.B ESDM_EGD_CLIENT_NET_CRL_ALLOW_EXPIRED
uses a CRL past its nextUpdate with a warning.
.RE
.IP
A TLS field in plain mode is an error, as is TLS without the certificate, its
key and the trust anchors, an address that does not parse, and TLS files that
do not load:
.B \-EINVAL
then.
.B \-EOPNOTSUPP
reports a build without the mode. A server that cannot be reached is no error,
as for
.BR esdm_egd_client_alloc ().
.TP
.BI "void esdm_egd_client_free(struct esdm_egd_client *" client ");"
Close the connection and release the client. Passing NULL is a no\-op.
.TP
.BI "const char *esdm_egd_client_socket_path(const struct esdm_egd_client *" client ");"
The socket path this client talks to \- in a network mode, the address of the
server. The string is owned by the client.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_egd_client_get_random(struct esdm_egd_client *" client ", uint8_t *" buf ", size_t " buflen ");"
Obtain
.I buflen
bytes of random data, waiting for the ESDM to become operational. Requests
larger than one protocol transfer are split transparently. On failure
.I buf
is cleansed, so a partial transfer can never be mistaken for a complete one.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_egd_client_get_random_nonblock(struct esdm_egd_client *" client ", uint8_t *" buf ", size_t " buflen ", size_t *" generated ");"
Obtain random data without waiting. The ESDM answers with as much as it can
deliver right away, which is nothing at all while it is not operational yet;
the amount actually delivered is stored in
.IR generated .
.I buflen
is at most
.BR ESDM_EGD_MAX_TRANSFER .
Use this only where a short answer is acceptable \- a seed consumer wants
.BR esdm_egd_client_get_random ().
.TP
.BI "int esdm_egd_client_get_random_pr(struct esdm_egd_client *" client ", uint8_t *" buf ", size_t " buflen ");"
Like
.BR esdm_egd_client_get_random (),
but served by the prediction resistance DRNG of the ESDM, which hands out no
more than its entropy sources just delivered: the answer takes as long as
collecting that entropy does. That DRNG takes a seed for each 32 bytes
.RB ( ESDM_EGD_PR_GENERATION )
a transfer begins, so a larger request is split into transfers of
.B ESDM_EGD_MAX_PR_TRANSFER
(224) bytes rather than 255: it costs as many seeds as it has 32 bytes, begun
ones included. The ESDM\-specific command this uses is unknown to
other EGD daemons, which close the connection on it \- the call fails then.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_egd_client_get_random_pr_nonblock(struct esdm_egd_client *" client ", uint8_t *" buf ", size_t " buflen ", size_t *" generated ");"
Like
.BR esdm_egd_client_get_random_nonblock (),
but served by the prediction resistance DRNG: the answer holds what its
entropy sources can deliver right away, often less than asked for.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_egd_client_get_random_unseeded(struct esdm_egd_client *" client ", uint8_t *" buf ", size_t " buflen ", size_t *" generated ");"
Obtain at most
.B ESDM_EGD_MAX_TRANSFER
bytes with the semantics of
.IR /dev/urandom :
from the DRNG, without waiting for all DRNGs to be fully seeded. Before the
ESDM is operational it hands out nothing, which is no error \-
.I generated
is 0 then.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_egd_client_get_state(struct esdm_egd_client *" client ", uint32_t *" fields ", unsigned int *" num_fields ");"
The state of the ESDM: the fields indexed by
.B ESDM_EGD_STATE_*
of
.IR esdm_egd_protocol.h
\- flags (operational, fully seeded), the entropy and the size of the
auxiliary pool, the write wakeup threshold, the maximum reseed interval.
.I num_fields
holds the room of
.I fields
on entry and the number of fields the ESDM reported on return, of which the
first ones up to the room were stored. A newer ESDM may report more fields
than the caller knows of.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_egd_client_status(struct esdm_egd_client *" client ", char *" buf ", size_t " buflen ");"
The status report of the ESDM as text, NUL\-terminated \- at most
.B ESDM_EGD_MAX_STATUS
bytes and the NUL. Returns its length, 0 if the ESDM had none, and
.B \-EMSGSIZE
if it did not fit, with as much of it in
.I buf
as did.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_egd_client_status_json(struct esdm_egd_client *" client ", char *" buf ", size_t " buflen ");"
The same as JSON document, except that a document that does not fit leaves
.I buf
empty with
.BR \-EMSGSIZE :
a document cut short is none.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_egd_client_entropy_count(struct esdm_egd_client *" client ", uint32_t *" entropy_bits ");"
Entropy the ESDM currently holds, in bits.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_egd_client_write_entropy(struct esdm_egd_client *" client ", const uint8_t *" buf ", size_t " buflen ", uint32_t " entropy_bits ");"
Insert at most
.B ESDM_EGD_MAX_TRANSFER
bytes into the auxiliary pool of the ESDM. The protocol allows attaching an
entropy claim to the data, but the ESDM only honors it for a caller running as
UID 0 and inserts everybody else's data without any entropy credit. An
unprivileged caller passing a non\-zero
.I entropy_bits
is therefore not an error \- the claim is simply dropped.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_egd_client_get_pid(struct esdm_egd_client *" client ", pid_t *" pid ");"
PID of the daemon serving the socket.
.SH RETURN VALUE
All functions returning
.B int
return 0 on success \- the status functions the length of the report \- and a
negative errno value on error.
.SH SYSCALL DISCIPLINE
On the UNIX domain socket, and in plain TCP \- not in TLS mode, see
.B NETWORK MODES
\- once connected, the operations stay within
.BR read (2),
.BR write (2),
.BR poll (2),
.BR rt_sigprocmask (2),
.BR close (2),
.BR getpid (2),
.BR clock_gettime (2),
.BR nanosleep (2),
.BR getsockopt (2)
and \- on a kernel without socket cookies \-
.BR getpeername (2),
the latter two to confirm before every operation that the descriptor still is
the connection made earlier.
That is deliberate: it is the set a seccomp confined process is typically still
allowed, OpenSSH's pre\-auth sandbox being the reference case. In particular
.BR send (2)/ recv (2)
are avoided \- they are
.BR sendto (2)/ recvfrom (2),
which that filter answers with
.B SECCOMP_RET_KILL
\- and
.B MSG_NOSIGNAL
is therefore not available:
.B SIGPIPE
is blocked around a write instead. Should the peer vanish right before a write,
the
.B SIGPIPE
it raises is taken off with
.BR sigtimedwait (2)
\- unless
.B SIGPIPE
was ignored when the connection was made, as it is in OpenSSH.
.PP
Establishing a connection is a different matter: it needs
.BR socket (2)
and
.BR connect (2),
which such a sandbox does not permit and does not merely fail but kills the
process for. A client that has to serve a sandboxed process must therefore be
connected before the sandbox is entered \- which is why
.BR esdm_egd_client_alloc ()
connects rather than deferring it. A process that forks into a sandbox cannot
be served at all, since the fork forces a reconnect.
.PP
.BR esdm\-server (8)
keeps such a connection open for as long as it sits idle, unless it runs short
of them: an idle connection is only closed once nearly all connection slots or
descriptors of the server are taken, or the UID of the consumer is being refused
further connections. Even then, the connections closed are those of the UIDs
holding the most of them, so a user who fills the slots cannot have the
connections of everybody else closed. A sandboxed consumer whose connection is closed
nevertheless \- that way, or by a restart of the daemon \- is killed by its
sandbox on the reconnect.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ESDM_EGD_SOCKET
Path of the EGD socket, used when
.BR esdm_egd_client_alloc ()
is called without an explicit one.
.TP
.B ESDM_EGD_TLS_MODULE
Path of the TLS module, instead of the one the library was built with. Not
taken from the environment of a process gaining privileges, as
.BR secure_getenv (3)
tells.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I @EGD_TLS_MODULE@
The TLS module, loaded by the first client in TLS mode.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
#include <esdm/esdm_egd_client.h>

int get_random(uint8_t *buf, size_t buflen)
{
	struct esdm_egd_client *client;
	int ret;

	ret = esdm_egd_client_alloc(&client, NULL, 0);
	if (ret)
		return ret;

	ret = esdm_egd_client_get_random(client, buf, buflen);

	esdm_egd_client_free(client);

	return ret;
}
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR libesdm_rpc_client (3),
.BR libesdm (3),
.BR libesdm_aux_client (3),
.BR esdm\-server (8),
.BR esdm\-egd\-net\-server (8),
.BR esdm\-egd\-net\-relay (8)
.SH AUTHOR
Markus Theil <theil.markus@gmail.com>
